course: dictionary
lesson: 7397
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and «pocket» is one of them. You can work out which meaning is right by reading the words around it. Those words are its context.
explain: «He felt sick eating them, and Dad found the wrappers in his pocket.» Wrappers are kept in a pocket, so here pocket means the small bag sewn into clothes. Always check: does your meaning fit the sentence? If it does, you have probably found the right one.
explain: Sometimes «pocket» is a doing word, a verb. To pocket something means to put it in your pocket or keep it, sometimes when you should not. A word can also describe a thing, like an adjective: a pocket torch is a torch small enough to carry in your pocket.
fact: A word can have several meanings, and context tells you which one is meant.
fact: «Pocket» (noun) means a small bag sewn into clothes, for keeping small things.
fact: «Pocket» can also mean any small space or area, as in «a pocket of air».
fact: «Pocket» (verb) means to put something in your pocket, or to keep it, sometimes unfairly.
fact: «Pocket» (adjective) means small enough to fit in a pocket, as in «a pocket torch».
exercise: «He felt sick eating them, and Dad found the wrappers in his pocket.» In this sentence, what is a pocket? | *A small bag sewn into clothes, for keeping small things | A loud noise in the street | A big hole in the road | A place for a kangaroo to sleep
exercise: «There was a pocket of warm air under the blanket.» What does pocket mean here? | To steal something | *A small area or space | A large building | A kind of coin
exercise: «She tried to pocket the spare coins.» What does pocket mean here? | Small enough to carry in a pocket | *To put something in your pocket or keep it, sometimes unfairly | A small bag sewn into clothes | A small space of air
test: Which meaning does pocket have in «a pocket torch»? | A pocket of air | *Small enough to fit in a pocket | To keep something unfairly | A small bag sewn into clothes
test: Which sentence uses pocket as a verb? | A pocket of warm air rose up. | The pocket was full of crumbs. | The pocket torch was very small. | *She decided to pocket the spare coin.
exam: Jayden says pocket always means a bag in clothes. Is he right? | *No, pocket can also be a verb, a small space, or an adjective | Yes, it only ever means a bag in clothes | No, pocket means a kind of animal | Yes, but only in maths lessons
